BOOKS['02']={title:'Economic Foundations',kicker:'Book 02 · The language of the economy',dek:'The concepts, accounts and market logic on which every later policy debate rests.',prev:'01-syllabus-roadmap.html',next:'03-growth-development-and-jobs.html',sections:[
{id:'choice',title:'Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost',html:`<p>Economics studies how people and institutions allocate scarce resources among competing ends. Scarcity does not mean absolute absence; it means wants exceed resources available at a zero price. Every choice therefore has an <b>opportunity cost</b>: the value of the best alternative forgone.</p><p>A production possibility curve (PPC) shows feasible combinations of two outputs with given resources and technology. A point inside is inefficient, a point on the curve is productively efficient, and a point outside is presently unattainable. Moving along the curve reallocates resources; an outward shift represents growth in productive capacity. Its usual bowed shape reflects increasing opportunity cost because resources are not equally suited to all uses.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Three efficiencies:</b> productive efficiency means least-cost production; allocative efficiency means output matches social preferences; dynamic efficiency means innovation improves possibilities over time. A market can be productively efficient yet socially undesirable if external costs, unequal purchasing power or missing public goods distort outcomes.</div><h3>Positive and normative economics</h3><p>A positive claim can in principle be tested (“a tax changes the price paid”). A normative claim contains a value judgment (“the tax is fair”). Policy analysis usually combines both: evidence predicts effects, while constitutional and social values guide the choice among them.</p>`},
{id:'markets',title:'Demand, supply and market equilibrium',html:`<p>Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices, other things equal. A price change causes movement along the curve; income, tastes, expectations, population and prices of related goods shift the curve. For a normal good demand rises with income; for an inferior good it falls. Substitutes are used in place of each other; complements are consumed together.</p><p>Supply is the quantity sellers offer at different prices. Technology, input costs, taxes, subsidies, expectations and the number of firms shift supply. Equilibrium is where intended demand equals intended supply. A binding price ceiling below equilibrium causes shortage; a binding floor above it causes surplus, unless the state purchases the excess.</p><div class="formula">Price elasticity of demand = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price</div><p>Elasticity depends on substitutes, necessity, budget share and time. When demand is inelastic, a price increase tends to raise total expenditure. Tax incidence falls more heavily on the less elastic side of a market—not automatically on the party legally required to remit the tax.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>UPSC lens:</b> apply this logic to MSP, rent control, fuel taxes, farm inputs, congestion pricing and sin taxes. Always distinguish the policy’s announced beneficiary from its actual incidence.</div>`},
{id:'failures',title:'Market failure and the economic role of the state',html:`<p>Markets coordinate decentralised information through prices, but prices may omit important costs and benefits. An <b>externality</b> exists when an action affects others without compensation. Pollution creates external cost; vaccination creates external benefit. Corrective tools include taxes, subsidies, standards, tradable permits and clear property rights, chosen according to monitoring capacity and distributional effects.</p><p>A public good is <b>non-rival</b> (one person’s use does not reduce another’s) and <b>non-excludable</b> (non-payers are hard to exclude). Free riding can cause under-provision. Common-pool resources are rival but hard to exclude, creating overuse risks. Club goods are excludable but largely non-rival until congestion.</p><table><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Why it occurs</th><th>Typical response</th></tr><tr><td>Market power</td><td>Seller can influence price/output</td><td>Competition policy, regulation</td></tr><tr><td>Information asymmetry</td><td>One side knows more</td><td>Disclosure, standards, insurance design</td></tr><tr><td>Externality</td><td>Unpriced spillover</td><td>Tax/subsidy, cap, rights</td></tr><tr><td>Public good</td><td>Free-rider problem</td><td>Public provision or finance</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination failure</td><td>Individually rational actions block joint gain</td><td>Standards, guarantees, public investment</td></tr></table><p>Government failure is also possible through weak information, capture, corruption, poor incentives or administrative overload. Good policy compares realistic institutions, not an ideal state with a flawed market.</p>`},
{id:'income',title:'National income accounting',html:`<p>Gross domestic product (GDP) is the market value of final goods and services produced within domestic territory during a period. “Final” avoids double counting; alternatively, sum value added at each stage. The three conceptually equal routes are production/value added, income and expenditure.</p><div class="formula">GDP = C + I + G + (X − M)</div><p>Here investment means addition to productive assets and inventories, not purchase of an existing share. Imports are subtracted because they may already appear inside consumption, investment or government spending. Gross includes depreciation; net subtracts it. Domestic follows territory; national follows normal residents. GDP at market prices differs from factor-cost measures because of product taxes and subsidies.</p><table><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Adjustment</th></tr><tr><td>Nominal GDP</td><td>Current prices; affected by price and quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Real GDP</td><td>Constant-price/volume measure</td></tr><tr><td>GDP deflator</td><td>Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100</td></tr><tr><td>GVA</td><td>Output minus intermediate consumption</td></tr><tr><td>NDP</td><td>GDP minus depreciation</td></tr><tr><td>GNI</td><td>GDP plus net primary income from abroad</td></tr></table><div class="warning-box"><b>Limits:</b> GDP is not a complete welfare measure. It omits unpaid care, distribution, leisure, ecosystem loss, quality change and many informal activities; defensive spending may raise GDP after harm. Use per-capita income, distribution, health, education and environmental indicators alongside it.</div>`},
{id:'flows',title:'Circular flow, saving and investment',html:`<p>Households supply factors of production and receive income; firms hire factors and sell output. Financial institutions channel saving toward investment. Government taxes and spends. The foreign sector buys exports and supplies imports. In accounting terms, one sector’s spending is another’s income and one sector’s financial surplus corresponds to another’s deficit.</p><p><b>Stocks</b> are measured at a point in time (wealth, debt, capital stock); <b>flows</b> per unit of time (income, saving, deficit). Saving is income not consumed. Investment adds to the capital stock. Ex post, aggregate saving equals aggregate investment through accounting adjustments, but planned saving and investment can differ, changing output and inventories.</p><div class="chain">Higher desired saving → lower present consumption → weaker demand in the short run if investment does not rise → possible fall in income → actual saving may not increase (“paradox of thrift”). Over time, saving that finances productive investment can raise capacity.</div><p>This distinction—short-run demand versus long-run capacity—is central to UPSC answers. A policy can support demand now yet create debt or inflation risks later; it can impose short-run adjustment while improving productivity later.</p>`},
{id:'measurement',title:'How to read economic data',html:`<p>First identify the variable: level, rate, ratio, index or share. Then check unit, base, frequency, coverage, nominal/real status and whether the value is seasonally adjusted, provisional or revised. A growth rate can rise while the level remains below trend; a sector’s growth can rise while its GDP share falls if other sectors grow faster.</p><p>Percentage-point and percent changes differ. If a rate moves from 5% to 6%, it rises by one percentage point but by 20%. Base effects can make year-on-year growth look unusually high after a depressed base or low after a spike. Per-capita averages can hide distribution.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Data sentence template:</b> “According to [source], [indicator] for [coverage] was [value/unit] in [period], compared with [benchmark]. This suggests [limited inference], though [measurement caveat].”</div><p class="source-note">Primary sources for updating: MoSPI national accounts and CPI releases; RBI Database on Indian Economy; Union Budget and Economic Survey.</p>`}
],recall:['Why can a policy improve allocative efficiency but worsen equity?','Distinguish movement along demand from a shift in demand with two examples.','Classify clean air, groundwater, a toll road and street lighting by rivalry/excludability.','Derive GDP by expenditure and explain why imports are subtracted.','Explain nominal versus real GDP, stock versus flow, and percent versus percentage point.']};

BOOKS['03']={title:'Growth, Development & Jobs',kicker:'Book 03 · Expanding capabilities',dek:'How economies grow, why development is broader than income, and how India can create productive work.',prev:'02-economic-foundations.html',next:'04-money-banking-and-inflation.html',sections:[
{id:'growth',title:'What drives economic growth',html:`<p>Economic growth is a sustained rise in real output; development is the wider enlargement of capabilities, security, freedom and sustainability. Growth can support development by expanding jobs and fiscal space, but its composition and distribution determine whether that possibility becomes reality.</p><p>Long-run output depends on labour, physical capital, human capital, natural resources, institutions and productivity. Capital deepening raises output per worker but faces diminishing returns unless complemented by skills, technology and organisational improvement. Total factor productivity captures how efficiently combined inputs generate output; it reflects innovation, infrastructure, competition, learning and state capacity.</p><div class="chain">Saving/finance → productive investment → larger or better capital stock → higher labour productivity → income growth → larger market and fiscal capacity. Leakages arise if projects are unviable, finance is misallocated, skills mismatch or institutions delay completion.</div><p>Potential output is the sustainable capacity of the economy; actual output fluctuates around it. A negative output gap signals unused capacity and weak demand; a positive gap may create inflation. Supply-side policy raises potential output, while demand management stabilises actual output.</p>`},
{id:'models',title:'Sources of growth and structural transformation',html:`<p>For General Studies, retain the policy intuition: investment expands productive capacity; diminishing returns make technology, skills and better institutions essential; and knowledge spillovers justify public support for research, learning and infrastructure. Mathematical growth models belong to Economics Optional and are deliberately excluded here.</p><p>Structural transformation moves labour and output from low-productivity activities toward higher-productivity manufacturing and modern services, while productivity within agriculture also rises. Urbanisation, logistics, reliable power, finance, market scale and women’s mobility shape this transition. “Premature deindustrialisation” describes declining manufacturing employment or share before a country reaches high income.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>India’s dual challenge:</b> enable workers to move to more productive activities while raising productivity and resilience where they already work. Agriculture cannot merely shed labour; cities and non-farm sectors must absorb it into dignified, productive employment.</div>`},
{id:'cycles',title:'Business cycles and macroeconomic stability',html:`<p>Economic activity moves through expansion, peak, slowdown/recession, trough and recovery. Demand shocks affect consumption, investment or exports; supply shocks change production costs or capacity. Automatic stabilisers such as progressive taxes and unemployment support soften fluctuations without fresh decisions, though India’s large informal sector limits their reach.</p><p>Investment is especially cyclical because it depends on expected demand, financing conditions and uncertainty. The accelerator links rising demand to investment; the multiplier links autonomous spending to successive rounds of income and consumption. Their size depends on spare capacity, propensity to import, tax leakage, financial conditions and confidence.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Do not label every slowdown a recession.</b> Examine multiple indicators—real output, employment, income, industrial activity and duration. A technical rule is a shortcut, not the whole economic assessment.</div>`},
{id:'development',title:'Development, capabilities and sustainability',html:`<p>Per-capita income measures average command over resources but not distribution or non-market freedoms. Human development combines a long and healthy life, knowledge and a decent standard of living. Amartya Sen’s capability approach asks what people are substantively able to be and do, shifting attention from resources alone to conversion factors such as health, gender, disability and public services.</p><p>Sustainable development meets present needs without undermining future generations’ ability to meet theirs. Weak sustainability assumes some substitution between natural and produced capital; strong sustainability recognises critical natural assets that cannot be replaced. A just transition distributes the costs and opportunities of decarbonisation fairly across workers, regions and consumers.</p><table><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Question</th></tr><tr><td>Income</td><td>How much command over goods?</td></tr><tr><td>Capability</td><td>What can people actually do or become?</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Who gains and who bears costs?</td></tr><tr><td>Sustainability</td><td>Does progress preserve future options?</td></tr><tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Can gains survive shocks?</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'employment',title:'Employment and unemployment',html:`<p>The labour force includes employed people and those not employed but seeking/available for work. The labour-force participation rate divides labour force by working-age population; the worker-population ratio divides employed persons by working-age population; the unemployment rate divides unemployed persons by labour force. A lower unemployment rate can coexist with distress if discouraged workers leave the labour force.</p><p>Unemployment may be frictional (between jobs), structural (skills/location mismatch), cyclical (weak demand) or seasonal. Disguised unemployment exists where some workers’ marginal contribution is near zero. Underemployment includes too few hours or work below skill/productivity. Informality concerns employment arrangements and social protection, not simply whether an enterprise is registered.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Measurement matters:</b> usual-status measures capture longer-term activity; weekly and daily status reveal intermittent unemployment and underemployment. Always state the definition before comparing figures.</div>`},
{id:'jobs',title:'India’s employment strategy',html:`<p>A jobs strategy must raise labour demand, worker capability and the quality of matching. Labour-intensive manufacturing, construction, tourism, care, logistics and modern services can absorb varied skill levels. MSMEs need reliable infrastructure, credit based on viable cash flows, technology adoption, market access and predictable regulation—not only classification-based concessions.</p><p>Supply measures include foundational learning, health, vocational pathways, apprenticeships and portable social protection. Women’s paid work depends on safety, transport, childcare, time burdens, norms and availability of suitable jobs. Urban planning is employment policy because affordable rental housing and mobility connect workers to opportunity.</p><div class="chain">Productive firms expand → labour demand rises → skills and matching improve access → wages and consumption rise → larger markets support further investment. Competition, formal protections and productivity keep the loop inclusive.</div><h3>Answer framework: jobless growth</h3><p>Define employment elasticity; diagnose sectoral composition, automation, informality, skills mismatch and female participation; distinguish quantity from quality; then propose an ecosystem covering sectors, firms, people, places and protection.</p>`},
{id:'demography',title:'Demography, migration and urbanisation',html:`<p>A demographic dividend is potential growth from a rising share of working-age population, not an automatic reward. It requires health, learning, jobs, savings, women’s agency and institutions. Without them, unemployment and social stress can rise. Ageing later shifts policy toward pensions, health and productivity.</p><p>Migration reallocates labour toward opportunity and diversifies household risk through remittances, but migrants may face insecure housing, documentation gaps and non-portable benefits. Portability, rental housing, public transport and recognition of skills improve both welfare and allocative efficiency.</p><p class="source-note">Update with PLFS (employment), MoSPI (national accounts), Census/population projections, Economic Survey and state-level evidence.</p>`}
],recall:['Distinguish actual output, potential output and the output gap.','Why do capital accumulation alone and GDP alone provide incomplete explanations of development?','How can unemployment fall because labour-market conditions worsen?','Build a five-part strategy for employment-intensive growth in India.','Explain why the demographic dividend is conditional.']};

BOOKS['04']={title:'Money, Banking & Inflation',kicker:'Book 04 · The monetary system',dek:'Money creation, banking balance sheets, RBI policy, inflation and the trade-offs of financial stability.',prev:'03-growth-development-and-jobs.html',next:'05-public-finance-and-budgeting.html',sections:[
{id:'money',title:'Money and liquidity',html:`<p>Money functions as medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Currency is money, but money also includes deposit claims usable for payment. Liquidity is the ease of converting an asset into means of payment without material loss. Broad monetary aggregates include progressively less liquid deposits.</p><p>Money has value because users expect others to accept it and the monetary-fiscal system preserves confidence. Fiat money is not redeemable for a commodity; legal tender concerns settlement of obligations, while practical acceptance also depends on payment infrastructure and trust.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Digital distinctions:</b> bank deposits are liabilities of commercial banks; physical currency and a central-bank digital currency are central-bank liabilities. A payment interface moves money—it is not itself money. Crypto-assets generally lack a sovereign issuer and stable unit of account.</div>`},
{id:'banking',title:'How banks create credit',html:`<p>A bank’s assets include loans and securities; liabilities include deposits and borrowings; equity absorbs losses. When a bank makes a loan, it typically credits the borrower’s deposit, creating deposit money. The system is constrained by capital, liquidity, regulation, funding cost, creditworthy demand, risk appetite and central-bank conditions—not a fixed mechanical multiplier alone.</p><p>Capital is the loss-absorbing cushion; liquidity is ability to meet near-term cash outflows. A solvent bank can face temporary illiquidity, and a liquid bank can still be insolvent. Maturity transformation—funding long assets with shorter liabilities—supports investment but creates run risk.</p><table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Control</th></tr><tr><td>Credit</td><td>Borrower fails to repay</td><td>Underwriting, provisioning, capital</td></tr><tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>Cannot meet outflows</td><td>Liquid assets, central-bank facility</td></tr><tr><td>Interest-rate</td><td>Asset/liability values reprice differently</td><td>Duration and hedging</td></tr><tr><td>Operational</td><td>Process, fraud, cyber failure</td><td>Controls, resilience, supervision</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'rbi',title:'RBI and monetary policy',html:`<p>The RBI issues currency, conducts monetary policy, manages reserves and public debt functions, regulates and supervises much of the financial system, operates payment systems and acts as banker to banks and government. Objectives can interact: price stability anchors purchasing power, while growth, financial stability, exchange-market conditions and government finance affect transmission.</p><p>The policy rate influences overnight money-market rates, bank funding and lending rates, asset prices, exchange rates, expectations and ultimately demand and inflation. Transmission is uneven because loans differ in benchmark and maturity, banks differ in balance-sheet strength, and informal borrowers may be weakly connected to policy rates.</p><div class="chain">Policy rate ↑ → short-term rates ↑ → credit/asset-price/exchange-rate channels tighten financial conditions → interest-sensitive demand moderates → demand-side inflation pressure falls, with lags.</div><p>Liquidity operations keep overnight rates aligned with the stance. Reserve requirements, standing facilities and open-market operations serve distinct liquidity and signalling roles. Monetary policy cannot cheaply produce food, fuel or semiconductors; it prevents a supply shock from becoming generalised through expectations and wages while targeted supply/fiscal action treats the source.</p>`},
{id:'inflation',title:'Inflation: measurement, causes and effects',html:`<p>Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, not a one-time increase in one item. Disinflation means inflation remains positive but slows; deflation is a fall in the general price level. Consumer indices track a household basket; wholesale/producer measures capture other transaction stages; the GDP deflator covers domestically produced final output with changing weights.</p><p>Demand-pull inflation arises when aggregate demand outruns capacity. Cost-push inflation follows input or supply shocks. Built-in persistence comes through expectations, contracts and wage-price behaviour. Food inflation depends on weather, supply chains, stocks, trade policy and market structure. Core measures remove volatile items to reveal persistence but do not imply excluded items are unimportant to welfare.</p><table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Possible effect</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed-income saver</td><td>Real purchasing power/returns erode</td></tr><tr><td>Borrower with fixed nominal debt</td><td>Unexpected inflation may reduce real burden</td></tr><tr><td>Poor household</td><td>High food share raises welfare loss</td></tr><tr><td>Firm</td><td>Uncertainty complicates price and investment decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>Nominal revenue may rise; indexed spending and borrowing costs can also rise</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'finance',title:'Financial markets and instruments',html:`<p>The money market handles short-term funds; the capital market channels longer-term debt and equity. In the primary market new securities raise funds; the secondary market trades existing claims, providing liquidity and price discovery. Bonds promise contractual payments; equity is a residual ownership claim. Bond prices and market yields move inversely.</p><p>Government securities form a benchmark yield curve. A steep curve can reflect expected growth/inflation or term premium; an inversion can reflect expected slowdown, but interpretation is contextual. Mutual funds pool investor money; insurance pools risk; pension funds convert present contributions into future income. Derivatives transfer or hedge risk but can amplify leverage.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Prelims trap:</b> buying shares in the secondary market does not directly create physical capital or give fresh funds to the issuing company, though liquid secondary markets support primary issuance and the cost of capital.</div>`},
{id:'stability',title:'NPAs, regulation and financial stability',html:`<p>A non-performing asset signals repayment stress under regulatory norms. High NPAs reduce interest income, consume capital and managerial attention, weaken fresh lending and may create a sovereign-bank feedback loop if public recapitalisation is needed. Resolution requires recognition, provisioning, viable restructuring, recovery or insolvency—not evergreen lending that hides stress.</p><p>Microprudential regulation protects individual institutions; macroprudential policy limits system-wide risk such as correlated exposures, procyclical leverage and interconnected failure. Deposit insurance reduces run incentives but can create moral hazard, making supervision and risk-based controls essential.</p><p>Financial inclusion has three layers: access, use and quality. An account without convenient transactions, suitable credit, grievance redressal or financial capability is incomplete inclusion. Digital finance reduces transaction costs but introduces fraud, exclusion, privacy and operational risks.</p>`},
{id:'policy',title:'Policy diagnosis and exam application',html:`<p>For any monetary problem, diagnose the shock before naming an instrument. Ask: demand or supply? temporary or persistent? broad or item-specific? domestic or imported? Are expectations drifting? Is credit transmission healthy? Who bears tightening costs?</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Inflation answer structure:</b> definition and measure → drivers (food/fuel/core, demand/supply/imported) → consequences → monetary response → fiscal/supply response → protection for vulnerable groups → medium-term productivity and credibility.</div><p class="source-note">Update from RBI Monetary Policy Reports and statements, RBI annual reports and database, MoSPI CPI releases, financial regulators and the Economic Survey.</p>`}
],recall:['Why are bank capital and bank liquidity not interchangeable?','Trace monetary transmission and name four leakages.','Distinguish inflation, disinflation and deflation.','Why do bond prices fall when yields rise?','Design a combined response to a food-supply shock without treating every price increase as excess demand.']};

BOOKS['05']={title:'Public Finance & Budgeting',kicker:'Book 05 · The state’s balance sheet',dek:'How governments raise, spend, borrow and share resources—and how to judge a Budget beyond headlines.',prev:'04-money-banking-and-inflation.html',next:'06-external-sector.html',sections:[
{id:'why',title:'Why public finance matters',html:`<p>Public finance concerns government revenue, expenditure, borrowing and intergovernmental transfers. Its classic functions are allocation (public goods and market failures), distribution (equity and social protection) and stabilisation (demand and resilience). A developmental state also builds capabilities, coordinates investment and corrects historical exclusion.</p><p>Fiscal choices face opportunity cost, implementation capacity and intergenerational effects. The right question is not whether spending is “high” or “low,” but what problem it solves, who benefits, its multiplier and long-term return, how it is financed, and whether delivery institutions can execute it.</p>`},
{id:'tax',title:'Taxation: design, incidence and GST',html:`<p>Direct taxes are imposed directly on income, profits or wealth-related bases; indirect taxes fall on transactions or goods and services and can be shifted through prices. Progressive systems take a rising share with ability to pay; proportional systems keep the rate constant; regressive burdens take a larger effective share from lower incomes.</p><p>A good tax system balances revenue adequacy, equity, efficiency, simplicity, certainty and administrability. The statutory payer need not bear the economic burden: incidence depends on relative elasticities and market structure. Tax expenditure—revenue forgone through exemptions or concessions—should be evaluated like spending.</p><p>GST is a destination-based value-added tax with input-tax credit across stages, reducing cascading when the credit chain works. India’s dual structure reflects federalism. Benefits include common-market integration and traceability; challenges include multiple rates, compliance burden, classification disputes, refunds and the treatment of excluded sectors.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Tax buoyancy</b> is the percentage change in tax revenue divided by percentage change in GDP, including discretionary changes. <b>Tax elasticity</b> attempts to remove the effect of discretionary tax changes.</div>`},
{id:'spending',title:'Public expenditure and quality',html:`<p>Revenue expenditure generally does not create financial or physical assets for government, while capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities. This accounting distinction is not a welfare ranking: teacher salaries and maintenance are revenue expenditure yet essential to make school assets productive. Capital projects can also be wasteful if poorly selected.</p><p>Evaluate expenditure through the entire chain: input → activity → output → outcome → impact. A higher allocation is not automatically higher spending; higher spending is not automatically better output; output is not automatically a durable outcome. Unit cost, targeting, timeliness, maintenance and independent evaluation matter.</p><div class="chain">Public infrastructure → lower logistics/time costs + crowding-in of private investment → productivity and demand → jobs and revenue. The chain weakens with delays, land conflict, weak selection, low utilisation or debt stress.</div><p>Subsidies can correct externalities, protect basic consumption or enable transition, but poor design may distort prices, benefit larger users, harm the environment or crowd out public goods. Prefer clear objectives, transparent budgets, periodic review and direct support where markets function.</p>`},
{id:'deficits',title:'Deficits, debt and fiscal rules',html:`<div class="formula">Fiscal deficit = Total expenditure − (Revenue receipts + Non-debt capital receipts)</div><p>The fiscal deficit is the government’s net borrowing requirement. Revenue deficit is revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts. Primary deficit is fiscal deficit minus interest payments and indicates the current-year imbalance before servicing past debt.</p><p>Debt sustainability depends on the interest-growth differential, primary balance, maturity, currency, investor base, contingent liabilities and credibility. When nominal growth exceeds the effective interest rate, a given debt ratio is easier to stabilise; this is not permission for unlimited borrowing. Productive borrowing can raise future capacity, while persistent borrowing for low-return spending creates vulnerability.</p><table><tr><th>Borrowing can help when…</th><th>Borrowing can harm when…</th></tr><tr><td>Economy has slack; project returns exceed costs; shock is temporary</td><td>Capacity is tight; debt is costly/short-term/foreign-currency; spending is recurrent and inefficient</td></tr><tr><td>It crowds in private activity</td><td>It raises rates, inflation or risk premium and crowds out</td></tr></table><p>Fiscal rules strengthen credibility but require transparent escape clauses for severe shocks and independent scrutiny. Off-budget borrowing and guarantees must be disclosed to avoid hiding risks.</p>`},
{id:'budget',title:'How to read the Union Budget',html:`<p>Begin with the macro assumptions and revised estimates for the current year, not only budget estimates for the next. Compare nominal levels, growth rates and shares of GDP. Separate tax and non-tax revenue, disinvestment and other non-debt receipts, revenue and capital expenditure, transfers, interest and major schemes.</p><ol><li>Read the Budget at a Glance and fiscal aggregates.</li><li>Compare Budget Estimate, Revised Estimate and Actuals over multiple years.</li><li>Adjust for inflation and GDP growth; large nominal increases may be modest in real or proportional terms.</li><li>Check composition: capex, health, education, transfers, subsidies and interest.</li><li>Identify financing, contingent liabilities and implementation constraints.</li><li>Translate measures into demand, supply, distribution, federal and environmental effects.</li></ol><div class="warning-box"><b>Headline trap:</b> a lower deficit ratio can occur because the denominator (nominal GDP) grew quickly. A scheme allocation can rise while falling in real per-capita terms. Always choose the appropriate denominator.</div>`},
{id:'federal',title:'Fiscal federalism and local finance',html:`<p>India assigns taxation and spending across Union, states and local bodies, but revenue capacity and expenditure responsibilities do not align perfectly. Vertical imbalance concerns differences across tiers; horizontal imbalance concerns differences among states. Tax devolution, grants and shared schemes address these while balancing equity, autonomy, incentives and national standards.</p><p>The Finance Commission recommends tax devolution and grants using criteria that can reflect income distance, population, area, ecology, demographic performance and fiscal effort. The GST Council embodies shared decision-making. Centrally sponsored schemes can promote national priorities but may constrain state flexibility or impose matching burdens.</p><p>Local governments deliver many visible public services but often lack predictable own revenue, professional staff and credible accounts. Property-tax reform, user charges with protection for basic needs, transparent grants and municipal finance can strengthen accountability.</p>`},
{id:'policy',title:'Fiscal policy in inflation and slowdown',html:`<p>In a demand slowdown with spare capacity, timely and targeted support has a larger multiplier, especially transfers to liquidity-constrained households and ready public projects. In supply-driven inflation, broad demand stimulus can worsen pressure; targeted relief, buffer release, logistics repair and credible medium-term consolidation are preferable.</p><p>Automatic stabilisers act quickly, while discretionary policy can be better targeted but suffers recognition, decision and implementation lags. Fiscal and monetary policy should be coherent: if one strongly expands demand while the other tightens, the combined cost rises.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Budget answer matrix:</b> growth | jobs | inflation | inclusion | fiscal sustainability | federalism | green transition | implementation.</div><p class="source-note">Primary updates: Union Budget documents (especially Budget at a Glance, Receipts Budget and Expenditure Profile), CAG, Finance Commission, RBI State Finances and state budgets.</p>`}
],recall:['Distinguish statutory tax incidence from economic incidence.','Why is revenue expenditure not synonymous with unproductive expenditure?','Derive fiscal, revenue and primary deficits.','List the conditions under which debt supports rather than weakens growth.','Use the eight-part matrix to evaluate a hypothetical Budget.']};

BOOKS['06']={title:'External Sector',kicker:'Book 06 · India and the world economy',dek:'Trade, balance of payments, exchange rates, capital flows and global institutions at the depth required for General Studies.',prev:'05-public-finance-and-budgeting.html',next:'07-agriculture-and-food-economy.html',sections:[
{id:'trade',title:'Why countries trade',html:`<p>Trade permits specialisation according to comparative advantage—lower opportunity cost—even when one country has absolute advantage in everything. Gains arise through larger markets, variety, competition, scale, technology and cheaper inputs. Their domestic distribution is uneven: consumers and competitive exporters may gain while import-competing workers or firms face adjustment.</p><p>Tariffs raise the domestic price of imports and produce government revenue; quotas limit quantity and create quota rents. Non-tariff measures include standards, licensing and trade remedies. Some correct safety or environmental risks; others disguise protection.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>GS answer balance:</b> efficiency and consumer gains → jobs and adjustment costs → resilience/security → fiscal and diplomatic effects → complementary skills, logistics, standards and social protection.</div>`},
{id:'bop',title:'Balance of payments',html:`<p>The balance of payments records transactions between residents and non-residents. The current account covers goods, services, primary income and transfers. The financial account records direct investment, portfolio flows, loans, banking capital and reserve assets. Accounting entries balance after errors and omissions and reserve changes.</p><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Goods</td><td>Merchandise exports/imports</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>IT, travel, transport, finance</td></tr><tr><td>Primary income</td><td>Interest, dividends, compensation</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary income</td><td>Remittances and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Financial account</td><td>FDI, portfolio, external debt, reserves</td></tr></table><p>A current-account deficit means domestic investment exceeds domestic saving and requires net foreign finance or reserve drawdown. It can be sustainable when financing raises productive capacity; it is risky when large, persistent, consumption-heavy or funded by volatile short-term debt.</p>`},
{id:'exchange',title:'Exchange rates and adjustment',html:`<p>With an exchange rate quoted as rupees per dollar, an increase is rupee depreciation; a decrease is appreciation. Depreciation makes exports cheaper and imports costlier in foreign-currency terms, but trade volumes respond with lags and depend on contracts, imported inputs and global demand.</p><p>Nominal effective exchange rates compare a trade-weighted basket; real effective rates also adjust for relative prices and indicate broad competitiveness. A real appreciation can occur through nominal appreciation or higher domestic inflation.</p><div class="chain">Capital outflow / higher import demand → demand for foreign currency ↑ → domestic currency pressure → imported inflation and foreign-currency debt burden may rise → RBI may smooth disorderly volatility, subject to trade-offs.</div><p>The impossible trinity says a country cannot simultaneously maintain a fixed exchange rate, fully free capital movement and independent monetary policy.</p>`},
{id:'capital',title:'Capital flows, reserves and external debt',html:`<p>FDI usually implies lasting interest and management influence; portfolio investment buys tradable securities and can reverse faster. External borrowing and deposits carry maturity, currency and rollover risks. Gross flows can matter even when net flows look stable.</p><p>Foreign-exchange reserves provide confidence, external-payment liquidity and capacity to smooth disorderly markets. Adequacy can be viewed against imports, short-term debt and broad external liabilities. Reserves have opportunity and valuation costs and cannot substitute for sound fundamentals.</p><p>External debt risk depends on currency, maturity, interest type, borrower and use. Currency mismatch—foreign-currency liability against domestic-currency income—amplifies depreciation shocks.</p>`},
{id:'strategy',title:'Trade strategy and resilient supply chains',html:`<p>Export competitiveness rests on productivity, quality, reliability, scale, logistics, finance, standards and market access—not exchange rate alone. Global value chains make efficient imported inputs important for exports. Rules of origin prevent simple trans-shipment but add compliance costs.</p><p>Import substitution can build capability where learning spillovers or security concerns exist, but indefinite protection weakens competition and burdens downstream users. Strategic support should be transparent, time-bound, performance-linked and paired with contestability.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Resilience is diversification, not autarky.</b> Map critical dependencies; diversify suppliers and technologies; hold proportionate buffers; build domestic capability where justified; retain global access.</div>`},
{id:'institutions',title:'WTO, IMF, World Bank and trade agreements',html:`<p>The WTO provides negotiated rules, transparency and dispute settlement across goods, services and intellectual property. Key principles include most-favoured-nation and national treatment, subject to exceptions. GS issues include agriculture support, public stockholding, fisheries, digital trade and development space.</p><p>The IMF conducts surveillance and provides balance-of-payments finance and capacity support, commonly with policy conditionality. The World Bank Group provides development finance, knowledge and risk instruments. Regional agreements cover tariffs, services, investment, standards and digital rules; judge trade creation/diversion, utilisation, sensitive sectors and strategic effects.</p>`},
{id:'shocks',title:'External shocks and policy response',html:`<p>Oil shocks affect the trade balance, inflation, fiscal choices, household income and exchange rate. Global monetary tightening can trigger outflows and raise funding costs. A partner slowdown weakens exports and remittances.</p><p>Match response to shock: allow orderly currency adjustment, use reserves against dysfunction rather than an arbitrary level, keep credible inflation/fiscal policy, protect vulnerable households narrowly and repair supply constraints. Temporary capital-flow tools may buy time but cannot replace fundamentals.</p><p class="source-note">Update from RBI balance-of-payments/external debt releases, Ministry of Commerce, IMF, WTO and the Economic Survey.</p>`}
],recall:['Explain comparative advantage without saying “cheaper.”','Why does the balance of payments balance in accounting terms?','How can depreciation initially worsen trade balance?','Compare FDI, portfolio flows and external borrowing.','Apply the impossible trinity to capital outflows.']};

BOOKS['07']={title:'Agriculture & Food Economy',kicker:'Book 07 · Farm to fork',dek:'Production, prices, water, markets, food security and rural transformation as one GS-III system.',prev:'06-external-sector.html',next:'08-industry-infrastructure-and-investment.html',sections:[
{id:'structure',title:'Agrarian structure and cropping patterns',html:`<p>Agriculture supports food, livelihoods, raw materials, demand and ecological services. Its employment share exceeds its output share, signalling low average productivity but not that labour can move without alternative jobs. Small holdings, tenancy, risk and uneven infrastructure shape choices.</p><p>Cropping patterns respond to agro-climate, water, soil, prices, procurement, technology, credit and demand. Incentives can lock regions into water-intensive crops. Diversification toward pulses, oilseeds, horticulture, livestock, fisheries and agroforestry needs storage, cold chains, veterinary/extension services and markets.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Map farm issues across:</b> productivity | profitability | resilience | sustainability | nutrition | equity.</div>`},
{id:'inputs',title:'Land, water, credit and technology',html:`<p>Land reforms include abolition of intermediaries, tenancy reform, ceilings, consolidation and secure records. Current needs include clear records without excluding legitimate users, fair leasing, women’s rights, dispute resolution and land-use planning. Digitisation helps only with correction and grievance systems.</p><p>Irrigation expansion without pricing, electricity reform and crop alignment can worsen groundwater depletion. Watersheds, rainwater management, micro-irrigation and local institutions improve resilience.</p><p>Credit is not income support. Waivers may relieve a systemic shock but exclude non-borrowers and weaken credit culture. Insurance, verified restructuring, income support, warehouse receipts and value-chain finance address distinct problems.</p>`},
{id:'msp',title:'MSP, procurement and farm prices',html:`<p>MSP is an announced floor signal for specified crops; effective support depends on procurement access and market price. Procurement supports stocks and prices but is concentrated by crop and region. A floor above equilibrium can create surplus; public purchase absorbs it but adds storage and disposal costs.</p><table><tr><th>Objective</th><th>Possible instrument</th></tr><tr><td>Income stability</td><td>Targeted income support, insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Price discovery</td><td>Competition, information, grading</td></tr><tr><td>Food stocks</td><td>Strategic procurement and norms</td></tr><tr><td>Diversification</td><td>Processing, demand and incentive reform</td></tr><tr><td>Risk management</td><td>Insurance, storage, suitable futures</td></tr></table><p>Market reform needs options plus bargaining power: mandis, collectives, digital platforms, direct purchase, grading, prompt payment and dispute resolution.</p>`},
{id:'risk',title:'Subsidies, insurance and climate risk',html:`<p>Input subsidies can promote adoption and food security but distort input mix, encourage depletion, distribute benefits unequally and crowd out research. Reform should be gradual, transparent and paired with viable alternatives.</p><p>Farm risk combines production, price, health, climate and policy. Basis risk arises when an index payout differs from actual loss. Timely data, transparent yield estimation, fast claims and public reinsurance for correlated shocks matter.</p><div class="chain">Climate volatility ↑ → income risk ↑ → risk-averse farmer under-invests → productivity stagnates. Irrigation + advisories + insurance + diversified income can break the trap.</div>`},
{id:'food',title:'PDS, buffer stocks and nutrition',html:`<p>Food security has availability, access, utilisation/nutrition and stability dimensions. Grain alone does not ensure diet quality, sanitation or absorption. PDS delivers subsidised staples through identification, allocation, movement and fair-price shops.</p><p>Buffer stocks support emergencies and stabilisation. Too little weakens protection; excess raises carrying losses and tightens open-market supply. Reforms include portability, tracking, social audits and grievance redressal.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Nutrition is not calories alone.</b> Connect diverse diets, maternal health, water, sanitation, care and evidence-based fortification.</div>`},
{id:'processing',title:'Food processing and value chains',html:`<p>Processing reduces perishability, adds value, smooths supply and creates non-farm jobs. Upstream needs quality inputs, extension, aggregation and traceability; downstream needs cold chain, standards, logistics, branding and markets.</p><p>FPOs can aggregate purchase, machinery, grading and bargaining, but require management, trust, working capital and actual buyers. Warehouse receipts reduce distress sale when certification and storage are credible. Futures aid hedging for suitable commodities but are not universal.</p>`},
{id:'reform',title:'Agricultural transformation',html:`<p>Move from output alone to income, nutrition and natural-resource health: invest in research; align crops with water; reform incentives gradually; diversify rural enterprise; strengthen competition and collectives; stabilise trade policy; secure tenancy and women’s rights; build climate services.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>GS answer sequence:</b> farmer objective → land/input/risk/market constraints → distribution/ecology → sequenced reforms → transition protection → measurable outcomes.</div><p class="source-note">Update from Agriculture Ministry, CACP, food/fertiliser departments, agricultural statistics, NFHS and Economic Survey.</p>`}
],recall:['Why can employment share exceed output share?','Evaluate MSP as price, income and food-security tool.','Distinguish food availability from nutrition.','Explain basis risk.','Design reform that raises income and saves groundwater.']};

BOOKS['08']={title:'Industry, Infrastructure & Investment',kicker:'Book 08 · Productive transformation',dek:'How firms scale, networks reduce costs and investment models turn public purpose into assets.',prev:'07-agriculture-and-food-economy.html',next:'09-planning-and-resource-mobilisation.html',sections:[
{id:'industry',title:'Industrialisation and structural change',html:`<p>Manufacturing can absorb varied skills, create tradable output, deepen suppliers and enable learning. Services can also scale, but employment intensity and skill needs vary. A strong economy connects agriculture, manufacturing and services.</p><p>Productivity depends on technology, management, skills, finance, infrastructure, competition and scale. A missing middle—many tiny firms and few mid-sized ones—may reflect fragmented markets, credit gaps, thresholds, land/logistics costs and risk.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Industrial policy</b> addresses learning, coordination, resilience or green transition, but risks capture. Use transparent metrics, competition, sunset clauses and export/productivity discipline.</div>`},
{id:'liberalisation',title:'Liberalisation and public-sector reform',html:`<p>Pre-1991 policy used licensing, public ownership, import controls and directed finance to build self-reliance but often weakened competition. Later reforms widened market allocation and investment, bringing productivity gains alongside regional, job and inequality concerns.</p><p>Privatisation transfers ownership; disinvestment sells equity; deregulation reduces controls; liberalisation widens market freedom. Public ownership may be justified by natural monopoly, security or public-service obligation. Ownership change without competition can replace public monopoly with private monopoly.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Evaluate via:</b> productivity, competition, investment, consumers, workers, regions, fiscal effect, strategic capacity and regulation.</div>`},
{id:'msme',title:'MSMEs, clusters and formalisation',html:`<p>MSMEs supply employment, entrepreneurship and regional dispersion. Constraints include delayed payment, working capital, technology, standards, management and market access. Clusters lower shared costs through facilities, skills and knowledge spillovers.</p><p>Formalisation is a continuum—registration, accounts, contracts, taxes and protection. It should bring credit, market and legal benefits, not compliance alone. Platforms expand reach but may concentrate bargaining power; procurement works only with timely payment and clear standards.</p>`},
{id:'infra',title:'Infrastructure economics',html:`<p>Power, transport, logistics, water, cities and digital networks raise productivity. High fixed cost and natural-monopoly segments require regulation. Pricing balances cost recovery, efficiency, universal access and externalities.</p><p>Economic viability includes time, safety, emissions, access and spillovers; financial viability asks whether cash revenue covers costs. Maintenance often beats visible new construction. Project appraisal must test alternatives and climate/disaster risk.</p><table><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Core GS issue</th></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>Grid balance, distribution viability, transition</td></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>Multimodal integration, safety, maintenance</td></tr><tr><td>Urban</td><td>Land use, transit, housing, municipal finance</td></tr><tr><td>Digital</td><td>Access, competition, privacy, resilience</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'capex',title:'Investment and stalled projects',html:`<p>Investment depends on expected demand, capacity utilisation, finance, leverage, certainty and execution. Low rates cannot revive capex if firms expect weak sales or banks fear loss. Stalls arise from land, clearances, litigation, finance, contractors, weak demand forecasts or coordination.</p><div class="chain">Healthy balance sheets + expected demand + certainty + infrastructure → private capex → capacity and jobs → income and demand.</div><p>Resolution needs early warning, credible contracts, time-bound decisions and risk allocated to the party best able to manage it.</p>`},
{id:'ppp',title:'PPPs and investment models',html:`<p>A PPP is a long-term performance-linked contract with significant private risk. It is not free infrastructure: users or government pay. Use it when outputs can be specified, lifecycle efficiency matters and risks are manageable.</p><table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Broad allocation</th></tr><tr><td>EPC</td><td>Government finances; contractor builds</td></tr><tr><td>BOT toll</td><td>Private finances/operates; bears traffic risk</td></tr><tr><td>Annuity</td><td>Government performance-linked payments</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid annuity</td><td>Financing and risk shared</td></tr><tr><td>Asset monetisation</td><td>Brownfield revenue rights exchanged for value</td></tr></table><p>Guard against optimistic traffic, land delay, hidden guarantees, renegotiation and weak regulation.</p>`},
{id:'green',title:'Green and resilient infrastructure',html:`<p>Assets lock in emissions and settlement patterns for decades. Appraisal should cover mitigation, adaptation and disaster resilience. Grids, storage, public transport and efficient buildings create capabilities but require finance and coordination.</p><p>A just transition supports workers, districts and consumers through reskilling, diversification and dialogue. Green finance needs clear taxonomy, disclosure and measurement.</p><p class="source-note">Update from sector ministries, regulators, Budget, Economic Survey, RBI and CAG.</p>`}
],recall:['Why can manufacturing create spillovers?','Distinguish liberalisation, deregulation, disinvestment and privatisation.','Financial versus economic viability?','When is PPP suitable?','Diagnose stalled projects.']};

BOOKS['09']={title:'Planning & Resource Mobilisation',kicker:'Book 09 · Coordinating development',dek:'From Five-Year Plans to cooperative federalism: mobilising finance, land, capability and institutions.',prev:'08-industry-infrastructure-and-investment.html',next:'10-inclusive-growth-and-human-development.html',sections:[
{id:'planning',title:'Why and how economies plan',html:`<p>Planning coordinates interdependent investment and social goals. Command planning can mobilise but has information/incentive problems. Indicative planning guides decentralised actors through public investment, forecasts and incentives. Markets discover information but do not automatically deliver equity or public goods.</p><p>India moved from capital scarcity, heavy industry and self-reliance through poverty priorities toward liberalisation, human development and federal coordination. NITI Aayog is a policy and cooperative-federal platform, unlike the former Planning Commission’s resource-allocation role.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Mission-oriented planning:</b> define a public problem, coordinate tiers and sectors, experiment, measure outcomes and adapt while preserving accountability.</div>`},
{id:'resources',title:'Meaning of resource mobilisation',html:`<p>Resources include tax, saving, public assets, credit, foreign capital, land, minerals, data, skills, technology, natural capital and administrative capability. Mobilisation brings them into legitimate, productive and sustainable use—not merely government revenue.</p><table><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Lever</th><th>Safeguard</th></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>Base and compliance</td><td>Equity, certainty</td></tr><tr><td>Assets</td><td>Better use/monetisation</td><td>Valuation, competition</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>Records, pooling</td><td>Rights, ecology</td></tr><tr><td>Saving</td><td>Inclusion, markets</td><td>Consumer protection</td></tr><tr><td>Natural resources</td><td>Transparent allocation</td><td>Community and restoration</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'finance',title:'Savings and financial intermediation',html:`<p>Households, corporations and government contribute to national saving. Banks assess credit and transform maturity; bonds connect issuers with investors; equity shares risk; development finance supports long horizons; venture finance supports uncertainty.</p><p>More finance is not automatically better. Stable macro policy, contracts, investor protection, competition and viable project pipelines connect saving to productive investment. Mispriced or directed credit can conceal bad projects and transfer losses.</p>`},
{id:'capacity',title:'Institutions and state capacity',html:`<p>Institutions are rules, norms and organisations shaping incentives. State capacity includes information, personnel, finance, coordination, implementation and feedback. Good design fails when workloads, procurement, data, grievance handling or local discretion are ignored.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Implementation paragraph:</b> responsible tier → financing → personnel → data flow → citizen interface → grievance remedy → audit/evaluation.</div><p>Digital systems need accurate records, privacy, interoperability, human fallback and correction. Simplification and professional capability often matter more than another scheme.</p>`},
{id:'federal',title:'Cooperative and competitive federalism',html:`<p>States differ in needs and capacity. Cooperation enables spillover management and shared standards; competition supports experimentation. Competition becomes harmful when states offer opaque subsidies or undercut labour/environment safeguards.</p><p>Good design provides clear functions, predictable finance, consultation, adaptation, comparable outcomes and dispute mechanisms. Local bodies require functions, funds and functionaries, not merely implementation duties.</p>`},
{id:'assets',title:'Land, natural resources and public assets',html:`<p>Land is asset, livelihood, habitat and identity. Acquisition must test alternatives, compensate fairly, restore livelihoods and prevent speculative capture. Pooling can share urban gains if participation and valuation are fair.</p><p>Mineral/spectrum allocation should be transparent, but auction revenue is not the only aim: competition, community rights and restoration matter. Asset monetisation transfers revenue rights, not necessarily ownership; it requires valuation, service standards and disciplined use of proceeds.</p>`},
{id:'evaluation',title:'Monitoring and evaluation',html:`<p>Monitoring asks whether implementation is on track; evaluation asks what changed because of it and why. Distinguish input, output, outcome and impact. No single method answers everything; combine credible causal analysis, administrative data, field evidence and citizen experience.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Policy cycle:</b> diagnose → objective → compare instruments → pilot/phase → implement with feedback → evaluate distribution and causality → scale, redesign or exit.</div><p class="source-note">Update from NITI Aayog, Finance Commission, budgets, CAG and parliamentary committees.</p>`}
],recall:['Compare command, indicative and market coordination.','Name six non-fiscal resources.','Why does finance not guarantee productive investment?','Write the seven-part implementation paragraph.','Monitoring versus evaluation?']};

BOOKS['10']={title:'Inclusive Growth & Human Development',kicker:'Book 10 · Growth that reaches people',dek:'Poverty, inequality, finance, health, education, gender and welfare delivery for Prelims and GS-II/III overlap.',prev:'09-planning-and-resource-mobilisation.html',next:'11-prelims-master-book.html',sections:[
{id:'meaning',title:'Meaning and dimensions of inclusive growth',html:`<p>Inclusive growth combines rapid, sustainable expansion with broad participation in production and fair access to its benefits. It is not redistribution after growth alone. It asks whether people have capabilities, assets, jobs, finance, infrastructure, voice and protection against shocks.</p><p>Pro-poor growth may mean poor people’s income rises in absolute terms or rises faster than average, reducing relative inequality. Inclusion has horizontal dimensions across gender, caste, tribe, disability, region and generation. Spatial inclusion connects lagging places to services and markets without erasing local rights or ecology.</p><table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Indicative question</th></tr><tr><td>Opportunity</td><td>Can people access quality education, health, finance and markets?</td></tr><tr><td>Participation</td><td>Can they obtain productive, dignified work?</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Are income, health and learning gaps narrowing?</td></tr><tr><td>Security</td><td>Can households withstand illness, disaster or job loss?</td></tr><tr><td>Voice</td><td>Can citizens shape and challenge delivery?</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'poverty',title:'Poverty: concepts and measurement',html:`<p>Absolute poverty compares resources with a fixed basic-needs threshold; relative poverty compares a person with prevailing social standards. Monetary poverty uses income or consumption. Multidimensional poverty captures simultaneous deprivation in health, education and living standards. Transient poverty follows shocks; chronic poverty persists.</p><p>A headcount ratio shows the share below a line but not depth. The poverty gap measures average shortfall; severity gives greater weight to the poorest. A line requires price updating and comparable consumption/income measurement. Administrative beneficiary lists serve programme purposes and are not automatically statistical poverty estimates.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Do not compare incompatible estimates.</b> Check poverty line, price base, survey method, recall period, spatial coverage and reference year.</div><p>Poverty traps arise when low income causes poor nutrition, learning, assets and risk-taking, which preserve low productivity. Public services, social protection, connectivity, credit and rights can relax several constraints together.</p>`},
{id:'inequality',title:'Inequality, mobility and social justice',html:`<p>Inequality can concern income, consumption, wealth, opportunity or capabilities. The Lorenz curve plots cumulative population against cumulative income; the Gini coefficient summarises its distance from equality, but different distributions can share a Gini. Top shares reveal concentration; Palma-type ratios compare top and bottom groups.</p><p>Some inequality may reflect innovation or life-cycle differences, but extreme concentration can reduce equality of opportunity, aggregate demand, social trust and political competition. Wealth inequality persists through inheritance, asset appreciation and unequal access to land, networks and quality services.</p><p>Policy combines predistribution (education, health, competition, labour institutions, asset access), redistribution (taxes/transfers) and protection from discrimination. Equality is not identical treatment: substantive equality may require accommodation or targeted support to overcome unequal starting conditions.</p>`},
{id:'finance',title:'Financial inclusion and digital public infrastructure',html:`<p>Financial inclusion covers access, active use, suitability, affordability and consumer protection across payments, savings, credit, insurance and pensions. Collateral-free or group-based models can widen credit, but over-indebtedness and coercive recovery must be prevented. Financial literacy cannot substitute for fair product design.</p><p>Digital public infrastructure can lower verification and transaction costs, enable portability and support innovation when identity, payments and data exchange are interoperable. Risks include authentication failure, cyber fraud, surveillance, exclusion and concentrated private power.</p><div class="concept-box"><b>Inclusion by design:</b> consent and purpose limitation; minimal data; offline/assisted channel; accessible interface; rapid correction; liability and grievance rules; independent oversight.</div>`},
{id:'human',title:'Health, education and human capital',html:`<p>Health and education are intrinsic capabilities and productive investments with spillovers. Markets underprovide preventive care and foundational learning because benefits are long-term, information is unequal and ability to pay differs.</p><p>Health policy needs public health, primary care, referral systems, financial protection, workforce, medicines and regulation. Insurance financing without adequate providers can inflate costs or leave rural gaps. Out-of-pocket spending can push households into poverty.</p><p>Education must distinguish enrolment, attendance, completion and learning. Early nutrition, foundational literacy/numeracy, teachers, language, assessment and school leadership matter. Higher education and skilling need quality, labour-market links and lifelong learning, not certificate counts.</p><div class="chain">Health + foundational learning → capability and productivity → better jobs/income → stronger household investment and tax base → improved services, if access and quality are broad.</div>`},
{id:'gender',title:'Gender, care economy and labour participation',html:`<p>Women’s economic participation depends on education and jobs plus safety, transport, childcare, eldercare, norms, property rights, digital access and workplace design. Unpaid care sustains the economy but is omitted from standard GDP when performed within households. Time-use data reveals this constraint.</p><p>Care infrastructure creates jobs and releases time for paid work. Equal inheritance/property, credit, collective enterprise, safe mobility, flexible but protected work and representation improve agency. Labour-force participation alone is insufficient; examine wages, hours, security, occupation and control over income.</p><p>Gender budgeting tracks how fiscal choices affect women; its value lies in changed design, allocation and outcomes, not classification alone.</p>`},
{id:'welfare',title:'Social protection and welfare delivery',html:`<p>Social assistance is non-contributory support; social insurance pools contributions and risks; labour-market programmes support employment and skills; public services build capabilities. A life-cycle system covers childhood, working age, disability, maternity, unemployment, old age and shocks.</p><p>Targeting concentrates resources but creates exclusion, identification and stigma; universalism reduces exclusion and can build coalitions but costs more. Hybrid designs—universal basic services plus targeted cash/protection—often balance objectives. Portability matters for migrants and informal workers.</p><p>Direct transfers reduce leakage where markets, accounts, cash-out and prices work. In-kind provision is stronger where the good has merit/public characteristics or markets are thin. Choice should follow context, not ideology.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Scheme evaluation:</b> objective → eligibility → benefit adequacy → delivery chain → coverage/exclusion → outcomes → grievance/audit → fiscal and federal sustainability.</div>`},
{id:'strategy',title:'An Indian strategy for inclusion',html:`<p>Build universal foundational services; pursue job-rich, green structural transformation; broaden productive assets and finance; empower women and excluded groups; make social protection portable; strengthen cities and lagging regions; and tax/transfer transparently without weakening growth incentives.</p><p>Measure inclusion through disaggregated outcomes, not average spending. Policy must handle intersectionality: a tribal woman with disability in a remote district faces overlapping barriers that single-category schemes may miss.</p><p class="source-note">Update from PLFS, NFHS, education/health surveys, household consumption data, NITI multidimensional poverty reports, budget outcome documents and parliamentary/CAG review.</p>`}
],recall:['Inclusive growth versus redistribution after growth?','Compare headcount, poverty gap and multidimensional poverty.','Why can equal treatment perpetuate inequality?','Design digital financial inclusion with safeguards.','Evaluate cash versus in-kind support contextually.']};

BOOKS['11']={title:'Prelims Master Book',kicker:'Book 11 · Recognition and elimination',dek:'A General Studies revision system: high-yield distinctions, statement traps and original practice questions with explanations.',prev:'10-inclusive-growth-and-human-development.html',next:'12-mains-answer-writing-manual.html',sections:[
{id:'method',title:'How UPSC tests Economics in Prelims',html:`<p>Prelims rarely rewards isolated memorisation. It combines a current term with a basic mechanism, reverses cause and effect, changes “may” to “always,” or places a correct institution beside a wrong function. Read the stem first, mark qualifiers, classify each statement independently, then choose.</p><ol><li><b>Definition test:</b> What exactly is the subject—stock/flow, level/rate, nominal/real?</li><li><b>Direction test:</b> Trace the causal sign; do not rely on mood words.</li><li><b>Scope test:</b> Is the claim always true or merely possible?</li><li><b>Institution test:</b> Who issues, regulates, recommends or implements?</li><li><b>Accounting test:</b> Does one side’s inflow correspond to another entry?</li></ol><div class="warning-box"><b>Absolute words need scrutiny, not automatic rejection.</b> “Only,” “all” and “necessarily” are often wrong, but a precise definition can legitimately be absolute.</div>`},
{id:'distinctions',title:'Fifty high-yield distinctions',html:`<table><tr><th>Do not confuse</th><th>Clean distinction</th></tr>
<tr><td>Growth / development</td><td>Real output expansion / wider capability and welfare change</td></tr><tr><td>Nominal / real</td><td>Current-price / price-adjusted</td></tr><tr><td>GDP / GVA</td><td>Final output at market prices / value added by producers</td></tr><tr><td>Gross / net</td><td>Before / after depreciation</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic / national</td><td>Territory / normal residents</td></tr><tr><td>Stock / flow</td><td>Point in time / per period</td></tr><tr><td>Level / growth rate</td><td>Amount / change in amount</td></tr><tr><td>Percent / percentage point</td><td>Relative change / arithmetic difference in rates</td></tr><tr><td>Inflation / disinflation</td><td>Prices rising / prices rising more slowly</td></tr><tr><td>Deflation / depreciation</td><td>General prices falling / currency or asset losing value</td></tr><tr><td>CPI / GDP deflator</td><td>Consumer basket / domestically produced final output</td></tr><tr><td>Repo signal / liquidity operation</td><td>Policy stance / quantity/price management of short funds</td></tr><tr><td>Capital / liquidity</td><td>Loss absorption / meeting cash outflows</td></tr><tr><td>Bond price / yield</td><td>Move inversely for an existing fixed-payment bond</td></tr><tr><td>Primary / secondary market</td><td>New issue / trade in existing security</td></tr><tr><td>Fiscal / revenue deficit</td><td>Borrowing need / revenue-account gap</td></tr><tr><td>Fiscal / primary deficit</td><td>Includes / excludes interest payments</td></tr><tr><td>Direct / indirect tax</td><td>Imposed on income/profit / transaction or consumption</td></tr><tr><td>Tax incidence / remittance</td><td>Economic burden / legal payment</td></tr><tr><td>Capex / productive spending</td><td>Accounting asset creation / economic judgment</td></tr><tr><td>FDI / portfolio</td><td>Lasting interest/influence / tradable financial position</td></tr><tr><td>Current / financial account</td><td>Trade/income/transfers / asset-liability flows</td></tr><tr><td>Currency depreciation / devaluation</td><td>Market movement / official parity change</td></tr><tr><td>NEER / REER</td><td>Trade-weighted nominal / adjusted for relative prices</td></tr><tr><td>Tariff / quota</td><td>Tax / quantitative limit</td></tr><tr><td>MSP / procurement</td><td>Announced support price / actual purchase</td></tr><tr><td>Food security / nutrition</td><td>Stable access to food / dietary and absorption outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Food subsidy / fertiliser subsidy</td><td>Consumer/stock system / farm-input support</td></tr><tr><td>Price floor / ceiling</td><td>Minimum / maximum legal price</td></tr><tr><td>Risk / uncertainty</td><td>Probabilities estimable / not reliably estimable</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance / income support</td><td>Risk pooling / transfer independent of insured loss</td></tr><tr><td>Privatisation / disinvestment</td><td>Control/ownership transfer / equity sale, possibly partial</td></tr><tr><td>Asset monetisation / privatisation</td><td>Revenue rights over brownfield asset / ownership/control transfer</td></tr><tr><td>EPC / PPP</td><td>Works contract / long-term performance and risk arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Financial / economic viability</td><td>Cash return / total social benefit-cost</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute / relative poverty</td><td>Fixed basic threshold / comparison to social standard</td></tr><tr><td>Headcount / poverty gap</td><td>Incidence / depth</td></tr><tr><td>Equality / equity</td><td>Same treatment / fairness considering need and barriers</td></tr><tr><td>Social assistance / insurance</td><td>Non-contributory / pooled contributions and risk</td></tr><tr><td>Access / inclusion</td><td>Availability / useful, affordable, safe participation</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'mcq1',title:'Practice set A — concepts',html:`<p><b>1. With reference to national accounts, consider:</b><br>1. Purchase of an existing listed share is counted as gross fixed capital formation.<br>2. Increase in unsold finished goods can count as investment.<br>3. Imports are subtracted in the expenditure identity to exclude foreign production already included in other components.<br><b>Answer: 2 and 3 only.</b> A secondary-market share trade changes ownership of a financial asset; inventory change is investment.</p>
<p><b>2. A fall in the inflation rate from 7% to 4% means:</b><br>(a) the price level fell 3% (b) prices rose more slowly (c) purchasing power necessarily rose (d) deflation.<br><b>Answer: (b).</b> This is disinflation; the general price level still rises.</p>
<p><b>3. If demand is much less elastic than supply, a commodity tax is likely borne mainly by:</b><br><b>Answer: buyers.</b> The less elastic side adjusts quantity less and bears more incidence.</p>
<p><b>4. Which can shift a PPC outward?</b> 1. Better technology 2. Increase in usable capital 3. Movement of unemployed workers into existing jobs.<br><b>Answer: 1 and 2.</b> The third moves production from inside toward the frontier.</p>
<p><b>5. Consider public goods:</b> 1. Non-rivalry means non-payers cannot be excluded. 2. Free riding can cause under-provision. 3. Groundwater is a pure public good.<br><b>Answer: 2 only.</b> Statement 1 defines excludability; groundwater is a common-pool resource.</p>
<p><b>6. Real GDP may rise while real GDP per capita falls.</b><br><b>Answer: Correct.</b> Population can grow faster than total real GDP.</p>`},
{id:'mcq2',title:'Practice set B — money and public finance',html:`<p><b>7. When a commercial bank makes a standard loan:</b> 1. A deposit may be created. 2. Bank capital automatically rises by the loan amount. 3. The bank acquires a loan asset.<br><b>Answer: 1 and 3 only.</b></p>
<p><b>8. Bond prices and yields:</b> For an existing fixed-coupon bond, market yield rises when price falls.<br><b>Answer: Correct.</b></p>
<p><b>9. A rise in the policy rate necessarily lowers food prices immediately.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Transmission has lags and cannot directly create supply.</p>
<p><b>10. Which deficit excludes interest payments?</b><br><b>Answer: Primary deficit.</b></p>
<p><b>11. Capital expenditure is always growth-enhancing and revenue expenditure never is.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Classification is accounting; project quality and complementary operation/maintenance matter.</p>
<p><b>12. GST input-tax credit primarily seeks to:</b><br><b>Answer:</b> tax value addition and reduce cascading, subject to an intact credit chain.</p>
<p><b>13. A fall in fiscal-deficit-to-GDP ratio proves nominal borrowing fell.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> The denominator can grow faster.</p>
<p><b>14. Tax incidence is determined only by which party remits tax.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Elasticity and market structure shape economic burden.</p>`},
{id:'mcq3',title:'Practice set C — external, agriculture and development',html:`<p><b>15. A current-account deficit:</b> 1. implies investment exceeds national saving in accounting terms; 2. is always harmful; 3. requires net finance or reserve change.<br><b>Answer: 1 and 3 only.</b></p>
<p><b>16. Currency depreciation necessarily improves trade balance immediately.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Contracts, volumes, elasticities and imported inputs create lags.</p>
<p><b>17. FDI is always less volatile than every other flow.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> It is generally stickier, but “always” overstates and intercompany flows can change.</p>
<p><b>18. MSP announcement guarantees purchase of all produce at that price.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Effective support depends on procurement and market access.</p>
<p><b>19. Excess buffer stocks can:</b> 1. raise carrying cost; 2. reduce open-market availability; 3. never affect farm incentives.<br><b>Answer: 1 and 2 only.</b></p>
<p><b>20. Basis risk in index insurance means:</b><br><b>Answer:</b> payout based on the index differs from actual individual loss.</p>
<p><b>21. A lower unemployment rate always signals better job conditions.</b><br><b>Answer: Incorrect.</b> Discouraged workers may leave the labour force.</p>
<p><b>22. Multidimensional poverty can fall even if a monetary poverty measure changes little.</b><br><b>Answer: Correct.</b> Health, education or amenities can improve independently.</p>`},
{id:'current',title:'Current-affairs integration without overload',html:`<p>For each economic news item, create a five-line card: <b>What happened? Which syllabus node? What mechanism? Who gains/loses? Which official source updates it?</b> Avoid separate mountains of current notes. Attach the card to the relevant concept.</p><p>Maintain one monthly dashboard with: growth/GVA, CPI, employment, policy stance, fiscal position, credit, current account/trade, exchange/reserves, agriculture and one human-development update. Record source, period and revision status.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Prelims filter:</b> retain a fact only if it clarifies an institution, classification, mechanism, geographic pattern, report/issuer or a major policy change. Discard commentary that cannot become a statement.</div>`},
{id:'revision',title:'Thirty-day revision protocol',html:`<ol><li><b>Days 1–10:</b> one core book per day; blank-page recall before reading.</li><li><b>Days 11–15:</b> all distinction pairs; explain each aloud.</li><li><b>Days 16–20:</b> 25 mixed MCQs daily; maintain an error log by cause.</li><li><b>Days 21–25:</b> revise only errors and weak causal chains.</li><li><b>Days 26–28:</b> two timed mixed tests; apply risk control.</li><li><b>Days 29–30:</b> sources, institutions, current dashboard and light recall.</li></ol><p>Error categories: concept gap, qualifier missed, institution-function confusion, direction error, overthinking, blind guess. The category determines the remedy.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Guessing rule:</b> decide based on expected value in your own tested accuracy band; never invent certainty from familiarity.</div>`}
],recall:['Explain five common Prelims trap designs.','Write ten distinction pairs from memory.','Re-solve questions 1, 7, 15 and 22 without seeing explanations.','Create a five-line current-affairs card for the latest RBI policy.','What does your error log measure beyond marks?']};

BOOKS['12']={title:'Mains Answer-Writing Manual',kicker:'Book 12 · Analysis into marks',dek:'GS-III frameworks, diagrams, question banks and model structures—without optional-subject detours.',prev:'11-prelims-master-book.html',next:null,sections:[
{id:'decode',title:'Decode the directive and demand',html:`<p>Underline the directive, content words, scope and any built-in claim. <b>Discuss</b> explores dimensions; <b>examine</b> investigates and reaches a finding; <b>critically examine</b> tests strengths, limits and assumptions; <b>evaluate</b> judges against criteria; <b>analyse</b> breaks into causes and relations; <b>comment</b> offers a reasoned view.</p><p>Convert the question into 2–4 sub-demands before writing. A strong answer addresses every word in proportion. Ten-mark answers need roughly two clear pages of thought, not a mini-essay; fifteen-mark answers need added depth, evidence and synthesis.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Opening options:</b> precise definition; current official context; constitutional/developmental purpose; short causal framing. Avoid generic quotations and long background.</div>`},
{id:'structure',title:'Universal GS Economics structure',html:`<ol><li><b>Frame:</b> define the issue and interpret the question.</li><li><b>Mechanism:</b> show how causes produce outcomes.</li><li><b>Evidence:</b> use one or two accurately sourced figures/examples.</li><li><b>Dimensions:</b> economic, social, federal, regional, gender, environmental and institutional as relevant.</li><li><b>Trade-offs:</b> acknowledge cost, timing, winners/losers and implementation.</li><li><b>Way forward:</b> prioritised and administratively feasible.</li><li><b>Conclusion:</b> answer the question with developmental purpose.</li></ol><p>Use headings that carry argument (“Why transmission remains uneven”), not empty labels (“Challenges”). A paragraph should make one claim, explain it and, where possible, support it.</p><div class="chain">Claim → mechanism → Indian example/evidence → implication for the question.</div>`},
{id:'tools',title:'Diagrams and analytical tools',html:`<p>Draw only what saves words or makes causality visible. Useful GS diagrams include circular flow, inflation transmission, growth-inclusion cycle, farm value chain, infrastructure crowding-in, demographic-dividend conditions and a stakeholder map.</p><table><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Issue tree</td><td>Causes split into demand/supply, short/long term or farm/market/state</td></tr><tr><td>2×2 matrix</td><td>Urgency vs importance; impact vs feasibility</td></tr><tr><td>Flowchart</td><td>Transmission and implementation chain</td></tr><tr><td>Stakeholder wheel</td><td>Distribution of benefits/costs</td></tr><tr><td>Lifecycle</td><td>Project, scheme or demographic stages</td></tr><tr><td>Map</td><td>Regional crops, industry, infrastructure or vulnerability</td></tr></table><p>Label arrows with verbs. A decorative circle earns little; “logistics cost ↓ → market radius ↑ → scale/productivity ↑” conveys analysis.</p>`},
{id:'evidence',title:'Using data, reports and examples safely',html:`<p>Prefer a small evidence bank from official sources: Economic Survey, Budget, RBI, MoSPI/PLFS, NFHS, NITI Aayog, CAG and parliamentary committees. Memorise indicator + approximate value/range + period + source; if unsure, use direction or proportion without false precision.</p><p>Examples should perform a function: demonstrate innovation, expose a bottleneck, compare states, or show implementation. A scheme name is not analysis. Explain the mechanism and limitation in one line.</p><div class="warning-box"><b>Currency discipline:</b> never present an old figure as current. Write “the [year/period] release reported…” and keep a monthly update sheet separate from evergreen notes.</div>`},
{id:'models',title:'Model answer skeletons',html:`<h3>“Inclusive growth is both an outcome and a driver of sustainable growth.” Discuss.</h3><p><b>Intro:</b> define inclusion as participation, capability and benefit sharing. <b>Body 1:</b> growth expands jobs/revenue; composition determines reach. <b>Body 2:</b> health, education, finance, women’s work and protection raise productivity, demand and resilience—feeding growth. <b>Body 3:</b> exclusions by region, identity, informality and digital gaps; trade-offs in targeting/fiscal space. <b>Way forward:</b> universal basic services, job-rich green sectors, assets/credit, portable security, empowered local bodies, outcome data. <b>Conclusion:</b> inclusion converts population into capability and legitimacy.</p>
<h3>“Capital expenditure is necessary but not sufficient for crowding in private investment.” Examine.</h3><p><b>Intro:</b> capex creates assets and demand. <b>Mechanism:</b> logistics cost and uncertainty fall, market access rises. <b>Limits:</b> poor selection, delay, maintenance gaps, weak demand, bank/firm leverage, policy risk. <b>Reforms:</b> credible pipelines, appraisal, land/clearances, contract enforcement, municipal/state capacity, maintenance and private-risk discipline. <b>Conclusion:</b> complement asset creation with institutions and utilisation.</p>
<h3>“Price support alone cannot secure farm incomes.” Evaluate.</h3><p><b>Intro:</b> MSP cushions price risk but effective coverage varies. <b>Benefits:</b> signal, procurement, food stocks. <b>Limits:</b> crop/region concentration, tenant exclusion, surplus/carrying cost, water distortion. <b>Package:</b> competitive markets, FPOs, storage/processing, insurance, income support, irrigation/research, stable trade policy and non-farm jobs. <b>Conclusion:</b> shift from price-centric to risk-and-value-chain strategy.</p>`},
{id:'questions',title:'Thematic GS question bank',html:`<h3>Growth and employment</h3><ol><li>Why has output growth not always translated into sufficient high-quality employment? Suggest a sector-and-place strategy.</li><li>Examine the conditions needed to convert India’s demographic transition into a dividend.</li><li>How do macroeconomic stability and structural reform complement rather than substitute for each other?</li></ol>
<h3>Money, inflation and banking</h3><ol><li>Food inflation requires coordination beyond monetary policy. Discuss.</li><li>Explain why healthy bank balance sheets are necessary but not sufficient for a private investment cycle.</li><li>Digital finance expands inclusion while creating new forms of exclusion. Examine.</li></ol>
<h3>Budget and resources</h3><ol><li>Fiscal consolidation should be judged by quality and credibility, not deficit reduction alone. Evaluate.</li><li>How can fiscal federalism improve both national standards and policy innovation?</li><li>Public asset monetisation requires stronger public capacity, not a retreat of the state. Comment.</li></ol>
<h3>Agriculture</h3><ol><li>Aligning cropping patterns with agro-ecology requires reform of prices, water, markets and risk together. Discuss.</li><li>Food security policy must move from cereal security toward nutritional security. Examine.</li><li>Farmer collectives can correct scale disadvantages but are not self-executing. Evaluate.</li></ol>
<h3>Industry and infrastructure</h3><ol><li>Industrial policy should create capabilities without protecting inefficiency. Suggest design safeguards.</li><li>Why do infrastructure projects stall, and how should risk allocation be improved?</li><li>Explain the economic and social foundations of a just green transition.</li></ol>
<h3>Inclusion</h3><ol><li>Universal basic services and targeted income support are complements. Discuss.</li><li>Women’s labour-force participation is an infrastructure and care-economy issue as much as a labour issue.</li><li>Multidimensional poverty improves diagnosis but does not eliminate the need for monetary measures. Examine.</li></ol>`},
{id:'review',title:'Self-evaluation rubric and practice cycle',html:`<table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Question</th><th>Marks signal</th></tr><tr><td>Demand</td><td>Did every directive/sub-part receive proportionate attention?</td><td>Relevance</td></tr><tr><td>Concept</td><td>Are definitions and causal directions correct?</td><td>Accuracy</td></tr><tr><td>Analysis</td><td>Are mechanisms, trade-offs and distribution visible?</td><td>Depth</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Is evidence accurate, current and functional?</td><td>Credibility</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Can an evaluator scan the argument?</td><td>Communication</td></tr><tr><td>Way forward</td><td>Is it prioritised, feasible and linked to diagnosis?</td><td>Judgment</td></tr></table><p>Practice cycle: untimed open-book structure → timed answer → compare against demand → mark missing mechanism/evidence → rewrite only weak paragraphs → revisit after seven days. Track recurring errors, not just scores.</p><div class="exam-box"><b>Final 30-second check:</b> directive answered? core term defined? both sides? Indian context? feasible conclusion? readable labels? no invented data?</div>`},
{id:'current',title:'Annual update protocol for GS',html:`<p>At the release of the Economic Survey and Union Budget, update one sheet per core book—not the books’ evergreen explanations. For each major change record: policy/indicator, what changed, stated objective, mechanism, likely gains, risks, implementation and source.</p><p>Monthly, add only exam-relevant RBI/MoSPI/PLFS/trade/agriculture items. Quarterly, delete superseded facts and retain trends. Before Mains, build a two-page evidence bank and one-page examples bank. This separation makes the library durable and prevents current affairs from burying concepts.</p><p class="source-note">This entire library targets CSE General Studies only. It deliberately omits Economics Optional’s advanced micro/macro models, mathematical derivations and history of economic thought.</p>`}
],recall:['Differentiate discuss, examine, critically examine and evaluate.','Build a seven-part outline for one question in the bank.','Draw three labelled causal diagrams from memory.','Which evidence sources will you update monthly versus annually?','Score your last answer against all six rubric rows.']};
